  "query_preview": "..\n\n...First measurable lowland snow possible Sunday...\n\nTonight through Saturday: Weather will be relatively quiet, with\nlimited snow shower chances winding down around Idaho, with \nthe potential for fog once again. This afternoon a broad low is \ndropping across the Canadian prairies and a shortwave on the\nbackside of this feature will be crossing over eastern WA and\nthe ID Panhandle, before pivoting out into early Saturday. Light\nsnow chances will linger across the northern and central\nPanhandle mountain zones; slight chances might also skim by the\nhigher Palouse. Otherwise passing flurries are possible over the\neastern third of WA this afternoon and tonight. Snow amounts\nwill generally be light, except toward Lookout Pass where a\ncouple inches of snow are possible. \n\nThe other feature tonight will be the potential for fog. The\nboundary layer remains moist and as we cool through the night,\nthe potential for fog will once again expand. The risk will be \nacross the Palouse, the Spokane/C'dA area, expanding west into\nthe Upper Columbia Basin and Moses Lake area overnight into\nearly Saturday as the easterly flow sloshes moisture westward.\nAs that easterly flow develops the fog threat wanes around the\nPalouse. Models have not been handling the fog placement and\ntiming very well, so confidence in details including precise\ntiming and coverage is fair. \n\nLow tonight will be in the 20s to lower 30s. Highs Saturday \nwill be in the mid-30s to low 40s. \n\nSaturday night through Sunday: A more robust system moves into\nthe area, with mountain snow and the a moderate to high \npotential for accumulating lowland snow, with some rain mixed in\ntoward the southern CWA. An occluding low pressure system\npresses toward the area Saturday night, with the boundary\npushing into the Cascades through southeast WA before lifting\ninto northeast WA and ID through Sunday. Snow chances come first\nto Cascades and western basin Saturday evening, expanding to\ncentral and southeast WA to lower ID overnight and become \nlikely across much of the region Sunday morning. There is some \ndisagreement on timing and amounts of precipitation. Something \nwe will have to keep an eye on it the shortwave coming in and \ninteracting with some unstable lapse to locally enhance snow. \nSnow amounts are generally between see 0.5 to 1.0 in the \nlowlands and up to 3 in the mountains. However locally heavier \nbursts of snow with any instability driven snow showers could \nbring locally heavier amounts. Mainly a rain/snow mix or rain is\nforecast over the deeper Columbia Basin and L-C Valley. Low \nwill be in the 20s to lower 30s and highs in the mid to upper \n30s.\n\nMonday through Friday: The pattern remains active with several\nwaves moving through. This will bring a chance of mountain snow \nand lowland rain and snow each day. The strongest system comes \nTuesday, which could bring moderate snows to the Cascades in the\nrange of 2 to 5 inches, locally higher in the higher peaks. \nOtherwise precipitation amounts will be lighter and trend to \nmostly valley rain and mountain snow Wednesday onward. High will\nbe in the 30s through Tuesday, warming to the upper 30s and 40s\nbeyond. /Solveig",
